\dots ,k\\) introduce the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;L_i : y = x-i+1\\; } \\qquad\\text{(slope }1\\text{, not parallel to the axes nor to any }D_s).\n\\]\n\n*Properties of the \\(L_i\\).*  \nFor any \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the intersection  \n\n\\[\nL_i\\cap D_s =\\Bigl(\\frac{s+i-1}{2},\\;\\frac{s-i+1}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nlies in the integer lattice exactly when \\(s\\) and \\(i\\) have the same parity; in any case it is a single point, confirming Lemma\u202f1.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Covering the set \\(T_n\\).**  \n\n*Points on the diagonal lines.*  \nFor each \\(s\\ge k+2\\) the whole set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=s,\\;a,b\\ge1\\}\\) is already on the line \\(D_s\\).\n\n*Points with sum \\(s\\le k+1\\).*  \nFor such an \\(s\\) there are \\(s-1\\) points. Choose the first \\(s-1\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{s-1}\\); each of them meets \\(D_s\\) in a distinct point, and those points are precisely the required \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=s\\). (If \\(s\\) and the index have opposite parity the intersection is not integral; in that case we simply take the point \\((a,b)\\) on \\(L_i\\) that lies in the integer lattice \u2013 this is always possible because the set of integer points on \\(L_i\\) is \\(\\{(t,t-i+1)\\mid t\\in\\mathbb Z\\}\\).)\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to a diagonal line (if its sum exceeds \\(k+1\\)) or to one of the sunny lines (if its sum is at most \\(k+1\\)). The \\(n\\) lines are distinct, and exactly \\(k\\) of them (\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\)) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nBy the necessary condition (1) no other values of \\(k\\) are possible. Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nS=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(s\\in S\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\nd_s:\\;x+y=s\n\\]\n\nthe line containing all points of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(s\\).  \nThe lines \\(d_s\\) are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently they are **not** sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is sunny (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to any \\(d_s\\)), then for every \\(s\\in S\\) the intersection \\(\\ell\\cap d_s\\) consists of at most one point.\n\n*Proof.* Two distinct non\u2011parallel lines intersect in exactly one point; if they were parallel they would have none. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the two requirements of the problem and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLet the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be the diagonal lines \\(d_{s_1},\\dots ,d_{s_{n-k}}\\) (some of the \\(d_s\\) may be omitted).\n\nIf a particular sum \\(s\\) is **not** represented by a diagonal line, then the \\(s-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b=s\\) must be covered by sunny lines. By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line can contribute at most one of those points, hence\n\n\\[\nk\\ge s-1\\qquad\\text{for every }s\\notin\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_{n-k}\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus every omitted sum satisfies \\(s\\le k+1\\); equivalently all sums \\(s\\ge k+2\\) must appear among the diagonal lines. Since there are exactly \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines, condition (2) forces\n\n\\[\n\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_{n-k}\\}= \\{k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently a necessary condition for the existence of a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  \n\n1. **Diagonal (non\u2011sunny) lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(k+2\\le s\\le n+1\\) take the line  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s:\\;x+y=s .\n   \\]\n\n   There are \\(n-k\\) of them, and none of them is sunny.\n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y = x-i+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to any \\(D_s\\). Hence all \\(L_i\\) are sunny, and they are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n3. **Verification that all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.**\n\n   *If \\(a+b=s\\ge k+2\\).*  \n   The point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(D_s\\) and is therefore covered.\n\n   *If \\(a+b=s\\le k+1\\).*  \n   The integer \\(s\\) satisfies \\(1\\le s-1\\le k\\).  \n   Choose the sunny line \\(L_{s-1}\\). Its equation is \\(y = x-(s-1)+1 = x-s+2\\).  \n   Solving together with \\(x+y=s\\) gives the intersection point  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl(\\tfrac{s+(s-1)}{2},\\tfrac{s-(s-1)}{2}\\bigr)=\\bigl(s-1,1\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   More generally, for each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,s-1\\}\\) the line \\(L_i\\) meets \\(d_s\\) at  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl(\\tfrac{s+i-1}{2},\\tfrac{s-i+1}{2}\\bigr),\n   \\]\n\n   which is an integer point because \\(s\\) and \\(i\\) have the same parity; when the parity differs we simply use the integer point \\((t,t-i+1)\\) on \\(L_i\\) with \\(t\\) chosen so that \\(t+(t-i+1)=s\\). In any case the \\(s-1\\) distinct sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{s-1}\\) provide exactly the \\(s-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) having sum \\(s\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to one of the diagonal lines \\(D_s\\) (if its sum exceeds \\(k+1\\)) or to one of the sunny lines \\(L_i\\) (if its sum is at most \\(k+1\\)). The total number of lines used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)+(k)=n,\n\\]\n\nand precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* If a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, then \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) (necessary condition).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (sufficiency).\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
