"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,n\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,n-1\\}, &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nIn other words  \n\n* the configurations with \\(k=0\\) (all lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) and with \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line) exist for every \\(n\\);  \n* if \\(n\\) is odd, a configuration with **all** \\(n\\) lines sunny exists, but a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines does **not**;  \n* if \\(n\\) is even, a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists, but a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny does **not**.\n\nNo other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n*Lemma\u202f1 (intersection bound).*  \nIf a line is sunny (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)), then it meets each diagonal line  \n\\[\nd_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (2\\le s\\le n+1)\n\\]\nin at most one point.  \n\n*Necessary condition.*  \nLet a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nEvery non\u2011sunny line can be taken to be one of the diagonals \\(d_s\\).  \nIf a diagonal \\(d_s\\) is omitted, its \\(s-1\\) points must be covered by sunny lines, and by Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line can supply at most one of them. Hence we must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge s-1\\qquad\\text{for every omitted }s .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently all diagonals with \\(s\\ge k+2\\) must be present.  \nSince there are exactly \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, (1) forces  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,s\\mid d_s\\text{ is used}\\,\\}= \\{\\,k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the points with sums \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\) have to be covered **only** by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Parity obstruction.*  \nWrite a sunny line in the form  \n\n\\[\na x+b y=c ,\\qquad a,b\\neq0,\\ a\\neq b .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (3) with \\(d_s\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{c-b s}{a-b},\\qquad y=\\frac{a s-c}{a-b}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHence a sunny line meets the diagonal \\(d_s\\) in an integer point iff  \n\n\\[\nc\\equiv b s\\pmod{a-b}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(m=|a-b|\\ge2\\).  Condition (5) shows that a sunny line can meet **only** those diagonals whose sums belong to a single residue class modulo \\(m\\).  In particular a sunny line can meet at most \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) different diagonals, because the smallest possible modulus is \\(m=2\\).\n\n*Counting argument.*  \nFor the sums \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\) we need respectively \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\) integer points (the numbers of points on the corresponding diagonals).  Using the bound above, a single sunny line can contribute at most one point to each of those diagonals, and it can contribute to at most \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) of them.  Therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\ge n      &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[1mm]\nk\\ge n-1    &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nTogether with the obvious lower bounds \\(k\\ge0\\) and \\(k\\ge1\\) (the cases \\(k=0,1\\) are realised below) we obtain the necessary condition\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,n\\}\\;(n\\text{ odd}),\\qquad\nk\\in\\{0,1,n-1\\}\\;(n\\text{ even}).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Construction for the admissible values.*\n\n*   **\\(k=0\\).**  Take the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(d_2,d_3,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\).  They are all non\u2011sunny and cover every required point.\n\n*   **\\(k=1\\).**  Use the same \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(d_3,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\) and add the sunny line \\(y=x\\), which contains the unique point \\((1,1)\\) of \\(d_2\\).\n\n*   **\\(k=n\\) ( \\(n\\) odd).**  \n    Write \\(n=2m+1\\).  For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n    \\[\n    L_i:\\;y=x-i+1 .\n    \\tag{8}\n    \\]\n\n    All these lines have slope \\(1\\) (hence are sunny).  \n    For a sum \\(s\\) the intersection of \\(L_i\\) with \\(d_s\\) is  \n\n    \\[\n    \\Bigl(\\frac{s+i-1}{2},\\frac{s-i+1}{2}\\Bigr),\n    \\]\n\n    which is an integer point exactly when \\(s\\) and \\(i\\) have the same parity.  \n    Consequently the \\(m+1\\) lines with odd index cover all points on the odd\u2011sum diagonals\n    \\(d_{3},d_{5},\\dots ,d_{2m+1}=d_{n}\\), while the \\(m\\) lines with even index cover all points on the even\u2011sum diagonals\n    \\(d_{2},d_{4},\\dots ,d_{2m}=d_{n-1}\\).  Every required point belongs to exactly one of the \\(L_i\\); hence all points are covered and all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n*   **\\(k=n-1\\) ( \\(n\\) even).**  \n    Write \\(n=2m\\).  Use the \\(m-1\\) lines  \n\n    \\[\n    L_i:\\;y=x-i+1\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,m-1)\n    \\tag{9}\n    \\]\n\n    (slope \\(1\\), sunny) and the \\(m\\) lines  \n\n    \\[\n    M_j:\\;2x+y=2j+1\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,m)\n    \\tag{10}\n    \\]\n\n    (slope \\(-2\\), sunny).  \n    The line \\(L_i\\) meets the odd\u2011sum diagonals \\(d_{2i+1},d_{2i+3},\\dots ,d_{2m-1}\\) in integer points, while the line \\(M_j\\) meets the even\u2011sum diagonals \\(d_{2j},d_{2j+2},\\dots ,d_{2m}\\) in integer points.  One checks that every point of the set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b>0,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) lies on exactly one of the \\(L_i\\) or \\(M_j\\).  Thus we have \\( (m-1)+m = n-1\\) sunny lines covering all points; the remaining diagonal \\(d_{n+1}=d_{2m+1}\\) is taken as the single non\u2011sunny line.\n\nThe constructions above realise all values listed in (7).  \n\n*Impossibility of the remaining values.*  \nIf \\(k\\) is different from the numbers in (7), then (6) is violated, i.e. the required number of integer points on the smallest diagonals cannot be supplied by only \\(k\\) sunny lines because each sunny line can meet at most one point on each diagonal and, by (5), can meet at most \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) different diagonals.  Hence no configuration exists for such a \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nd_s:\\;x+y=s\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1).\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) consists of the points of the diagonals \\(d_2,d_3,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\);  \n\\(|d_s|=s-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is sunny (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)), then for every \\(s\\) the intersection \\(\\ell\\cap d_s\\) consists of at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  Two distinct non\u2011parallel lines intersect in exactly one point; if they were parallel they would have none.  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLet the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be the diagonals \\(d_{s_1},\\dots ,d_{s_{\\,n-k}}\\) (they may be taken to be parallel to the forbidden directions, because any non\u2011sunny line can be replaced by a parallel diagonal without losing covered points).\n\nIf a diagonal \\(d_s\\) is **omitted**, its \\(s-1\\) points must be covered by sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line can contribute at most one of those points, therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\ge s-1\\qquad\\text{for every omitted }s. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently all diagonals with \\(s\\ge k+2\\) must be present; otherwise (1) would be violated.  \nSince there are exactly \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, (1) forces\n\n\\[\n\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_{\\,n-k}\\}= \\{k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the points belonging to the diagonals \\(d_2,d_3,\\dots ,d_{k+1}\\) have to be covered **only** by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n------------------------------------------